# orgitorial — authoring

Read this when you are *writing post content* in org for orgitorial to render. It is the map of the supported subset — what to type and what it becomes. If you can write a to-do list, you already know enough. For how the rendered classes are styled, see `components`; for plugging the output into a site, see `integrate`.

app :orgitorial__authoring do
  title       "orgitorial — authoring"
  description "Read this when you are *writing post content* in org for orgitorial to render."
end

## The mental model

A post is one `.org` file: a metadata block at the top, then prose organized by `*` headlines. orgitorial supports the *blogging 90%* of org — the constructs you actually reach for in an article. Anything outside the subset is *not dropped*: it is preserved as escaped text in a `.org-raw` block so nothing silently vanishes. Malformed markup never breaks the render; it degrades to a paragraph.

## Metadata (the header)

Put keyword lines at the top. They become the article `<header>` (unless you render with `header:false`) and are also returned by `parseMeta` for page titles / OG tags / indexes.

```org
#+TITLE: Writing for the Web in Org-mode
#+AUTHOR: The Orgitorial Team
#+DATE: 2031-11-22
#+keywords: org-mode, writing, publishing
```

- `#+TITLE:` → `h1.org-title`
- `#+AUTHOR:` → `.org-author`
- `#+DATE:` → `.org-date` (any string; an ISO date also feeds inline
timestamps elsewhere)
- `#+keywords:` (or `#+tags:`) → comma/space separated → tag pills in the header

Other `#+keyword:` lines you don't need are ignored safely.

## Headlines, TODO state, and tags

Stars set depth. org level 1 renders as `h2` (`h1` is reserved for the title), levels 2–4 as `h3`–`h5`.

```org
* Section                     -> h2.org-h
** Subsection                 -> h3.org-h
*** Sub-subsection            -> h4.org-h
* TODO Ship the renderer      -> state chip "TODO"  (h2.org-h.org-todo)
* DONE Editorial CSS          -> state chip "DONE"  (h2.org-h.org-done)
* Roadmap :planning:urgent:   -> trailing :tags: become pills
```

Recognized TODO keywords: `TODO NEXT WAITING STARTED IN-PROGRESS` (rendered as `org-todo`); DONE keywords: `DONE CANCELLED CANCELED` (`org-done`). They must be ALL-CAPS and lead the headline. Tags are `:colon:delimited:` at the line end.

## Inline markup

| You type        | You get             | Class               | |-----------------|---------------------|---------------------| | `*bold*`        | *bold*              | `strong`            | | `/italic/`      | /italic/            | `em`                | | `_underline_`   | underline           | `u`                 | | `+strike+`      | strike              | `del`               | | =`verbatim`=    | literal text        | `code.org-verbatim` | | ``code``        | a code token        | `code.org-code`     |

Emphasis follows org's rule: the marker hugs the word (no space just inside), and is bounded by whitespace or punctuation. `a * b` is not bold.

## Links, images, timestamps

```org
[[https://orgmode.org][the org-mode site]]   -> a.org-link with description
[[https://workbooks.sh]]                       -> a.org-link, url as text
https://workbooks.sh                           -> bare url auto-links
[[diagram.png]]                                -> img.org-img (image extensions)
[[photo.jpg][A caption]]                        -> img with alt="A caption"
<2031-11-22 Sat>                               -> time.org-ts (inline timestamp)
```

Image extensions detected: png jpg jpeg gif svg webp avif bmp.

## Lists and checkboxes

Indent two spaces to nest. Mix is fine.

```org
- unordered item              (dash, plus, or star bullet)
- second
  - nested (indent 2)
1. ordered item               (N. or N) )
2. second

- [ ] not done                -> li.org-check[data-checked="false"]
- [X] done                    -> li.org-check[data-checked="true"]
- [-] partial                 -> li.org-check[data-checked="partial"]
```

## Tables

Pipe-delimited. A rule line (`|---|`, any `|-+-|` shape) marks the row *above* it as the header.

```org
| Name  | Role     |
|-------+----------|
| Ada   | author   |
| Linus | reviewer |
```

## Blocks

```org
,#+begin_src python          -> pre.org-src[data-lang="python"], escaped,
print("hi")                     code.org-src-code.language-python
,#+end_src

,#+begin_quote                -> blockquote.org-quote
A line worth quoting.
,#+end_quote

,#+begin_example              -> pre.org-example (literal, no language bar)
raw output, preserved
,#+end_example

,#+begin_verse                -> p.org-verse (line breaks preserved)
Roses are red
Violets are blue
,#+end_verse
```

(The leading comma above is org's escape for a nested block in a source block — drop it when you write a real block.) Source blocks escape HTML and label the language; bring your own highlighter by targeting `.org-src-code.language-*`.

## Living blocks: when an article should also be a workbook

Every article *can* be a workbook. You have freedom to build custom components inline. Reach for one of these only when it earns its place — when a picture or 30 seconds of interaction genuinely beats the paragraph:

- *A diagram that beats a paragraph.* If you're describing a flow, a pipeline,
a state machine — draw it. `#+begin_src mermaid` renders a real diagram.
- *A 30-second interactive that beats a chart.* A slider the reader drags, a
tiny estimator, a toggle that shows the difference — a `:app` block lets them feel the point instead of reading it.

Two hard rules from the start: keep mini-apps *condensed* (a self-contained widget, not a whole SPA), and *never let a block inner-scroll* — no cramped, scrolling iframe. A block can go *full width* instead of being crammed into the editorial column (`:width`, below).

### Mermaid diagram

```org
,#+begin_src mermaid          -> div.org-mermaid (rendered by the host's activate())
flowchart LR
  A[".org"] --> B["render()"] --> C["page"]
,#+end_src
```

Write plain mermaid. The page renders it *only if* it calls `Orgitorial.activate(document)` (see `integrate`); the renderer itself just carries the text, so output stays zero-dependency.

### Live mini-app (`:app`)

A `#+begin_src html :app` block is your own HTML, inline and *live* — its `<script>` runs when the host calls `activate()`. This is first-party content (you authored it), same trust model as any HTML you write into the page.

```org
,#+begin_src html :app :width full
<div style="text-align:center">
  <input id="n" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="20">
  <output id="o">20</output>
  <script>n.oninput = () => o.textContent = n.value;<\/script>
</div>
,#+end_src
```

Keep it self-contained: inline styles, an inline script, no external build. A module script *may* `import` wasm (e.g. a small candle-compiled model) — keep the payload lean. `#+begin_export html` is the same live surface for when you don't want a language tag.

### Width: break out of the column

Add `:width wide` or `:width full` to *any* block (src, quote, `:app`, mermaid):

```org
,#+begin_quote :width wide        -> breaks past the measure, capped to the viewport
A pull-quote that wants room.
,#+end_quote

,#+begin_src html :app :width full  -> edge-to-edge full-bleed
…
,#+end_src
```

Default is `column` (stays in the measure). Use `wide` for figures and pull-quotes, `full` for a hero-scale interactive.

## Drawers, footnotes, rules

```org
:PROPERTIES:                  -> details.org-drawer (collapsed by default)
:ID:       my-post
:CATEGORY: tutorial
:END:

Text with a note[fn:1].       -> sup.org-fnref linking to the definition

[fn:1] The definition.        -> collected into a Footnotes section at the end

-----                         -> hr.org-rule  (5+ dashes on their own line)
```

## What is NOT in the subset (and what happens)

Consciously excluded — these pass through as escaped `.org-raw`, never lost:

- `#+begin_src` babel *execution / results* (`:results`, `#+RESULTS:`) — no code
runs at render time. (A `:app` block is different: its HTML is live in the browser via `activate()` — that's an authored mini-app, not babel evaluation.)
- *LaTeX math* (`\(...\)`, `$...$`, `#+begin_math`) — passes through verbatim.
- `#+INCLUDE:`, macros (`#+MACRO:` / `{{{...}}}`), babel noweb references.
- *Radio targets*, internal `<<targets>>` / `[*Headline](*Headline)` cross-links,
`#+NAME:`/`#+CAPTION:` affiliated keywords.
- Description lists (`term :: def`), `#+begin_center/comment`, clock/scheduling
drawers, column view.
- *Subscripts/superscripts* (`a^2`, `H_2O`) and entities (`\alpha`).

If you need one of these, it is a deliberate scope decision — author around it, or extend the renderer (and update `components`). The contract: unsupported ≠ silent. You will see it preserved, escaped, in the output.

## Done-when

The post reads as you intend, every construct you used is in the subset table above (or you accept it rendering as `.org-raw`), and `parseMeta` returns the title/author/date you set. Verify by rendering once — don't guess.